  Next question, and it's the one with a security consequence, so I want your answer before I shape anything.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the stored value is just a label the UI displays, or something later requests rely on to prove who
  the caller is. Those are the same three characters on screen and completely different systems underneath.

  - [1mDisplay/tracking only[0m — the ID identifies the user in log lines and UI ("logged in as 4417"), and anything the
    server does with it, the server re-derives from its own session. Non-secret, so browser storage is fine and the
    design stays small. Nothing to redo later.
  - [1mOther forms send it as the identity[0m — later forms read the ID from the store and submit it, and the server trusts
    it. This is the shape your "other forms will need it later" could imply, and I'd push back on it: any user can edit
    browser storage, so a server that trusts a client-supplied [38;5;153muserId[39m lets anyone act as anyone. If that's the intent,
    the fix belongs on the server (a session cookie or token the client never chooses), and the client store goes back
    to being display-only.
  - [1mStore a session token alongside the ID[0m — the store holds the credential too. Makes the client the keeper of a
    secret, which means the storage choice becomes an XSS exposure decision rather than a convenience one. Real systems
    do this, but it's a meaningfully larger design and it should be a deliberate call, not a side effect of adding a
    parameter.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m display/tracking only. It matches what you originally asked for, it's the only one of the three
  that's cheap to get wrong, and it doesn't quietly build an auth mechanism out of a logging change. If you need the
  second or third, say so and I'll design it properly rather than approximating it.
